<%# Source: https://docs.fulcrumapp.com/reference/query-post %>
<%# Fragment: paste into a Report Template; this is not a standalone template. %>
<%# Purpose: encode a user-controlled parameter before it reaches SQL. %>
<%#
  The Query endpoint accepts one complete SQL string in `q` and exposes no
  server-side bind parameters, so the template is responsible for encoding.
  Reduce the value to an allowlisted character class before interpolating it.

  Encode in the gap itself, not in a variable further up. A reader — and any
  checker — can see that `('' + status).replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9_-]/g, '')` yields
  letters, digits, underscores, and hyphens, and that none of those can close
  the quote around them. The same filter written twenty lines earlier proves
  nothing at the point where the value reaches SQL, because the name in the gap
  could have been reassigned in between.

  `('' + value)` converts without naming `String`. That matters: `String` is an
  ordinary binding inside a template, so `catch (String)` or a parameter of that
  name would silently change what a `String(value)` conversion means, while an
  empty-string concatenation has no name to rebind.
%>

<% const status = $params.status || ''; %>
<% const result = QUERY(
  `SELECT * FROM "App" WHERE _status = '${('' + status).replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9_-]/g, '')}'`,
  { format: 'json' }
); %>
